\details{
\strong{What the Trafa API states, and how.} The structure endpoint answers one
selection at a time: given a product, a measure and some dimensions, every
item of the product comes back with an \code{Option} flag that says whether it
can still be added. It publishes no list of allowed combinations. A
selection that cannot be combined comes back with every flag \code{FALSE}, and
the \emph{data} endpoint does not refuse it: it answers with no rows, or with a
lone total row for a dimension it cannot break down by. A dimension lists
the same values whatever the measure, so the structure says nothing about
which \emph{values} hold data.

\strong{What is returned.} For each measure, its largest allowed sets of
dimensions (\code{sets}). A selection is allowed when it is part of one of
them. In practice a set is one of the tables the product is built from.

\strong{How this function asks.} One request for the product; per measure one
request for the dimensions it can take, one per such dimension for what
can be chosen beside it (also the verdict on that dimension with the
measure and the year), and then one per candidate set of three or more
dimensions (the largest groups of dimensions that can all be paired with
each other; a candidate
the API turns down is tried again with one dimension less). This rests on
one assumption: a part of an allowed set is itself allowed. The package's
tests compare the result with the exhaustive walk of
\code{\link[=discover_allowed_combinations]{discover_allowed_combinations()}} on recorded products, where they agree.
The exhaustive walk needs one request per allowed set, for a large product
more than a thousand; this function needs a fraction of that.

The time dimension \code{ar}, which every data request must carry, is part of
every request made here when the measure can take it, and of every set.

\strong{Structure is not data.} A combination the structure allows can still
hold no rows; see \code{verify} in \code{\link[=discover_allowed_combinations]{discover_allowed_combinations()}}.
